\chapter{Android and the Many Meanings of Unix}
\label{ch:android}
\index{Android}\index{Bionic}\index{Binder}\index{Darwin}\index{macOS}

\section{The phone does not need to look like its kernel}

Android's first commercial phones appeared in 2008.
A user encountered applications, touch interaction, a lifecycle managed
by the platform, and services designed around a mobile device.
Underneath, the Linux kernel supplied process isolation, memory
management, scheduling, networking, drivers, and other essential
mechanisms. The application experience did not have to resemble a
Linux workstation to benefit from that machinery.

This distinction is the reason the title ends with Android.
The continuity is substantial, but it is not the survival of an unchanged
user interface. A phone owner may never see a shell or a pathname.
The abstractions that made Unix useful to programmers can operate behind
interfaces designed for entirely different habits and constraints.

Mobile devices also change the operating environment.
Energy, thermal limits, radios, cameras, sensors, and intermittent
connectivity are central concerns. A background computation can reduce
battery life or interfere with an immediate interaction.
An application may need to stop and restart under platform control rather
than assume it owns an uninterrupted session.

The kernel is therefore a foundation, not a complete explanation of the
product. Understanding Android requires following the layers above it
and the institutions that decide how those layers are assembled.

\section{Bionic marks a different userspace}

Conventional GNU/Linux distributions commonly use the GNU C library and
many GNU utilities. Android uses Bionic as its C library, math library,
and dynamic-linker implementation. The official maintainer overview
identifies these components explicitly and describes the loader's role
in starting ELF programs \parencite{bionic}.

The choice affects binary compatibility. A native executable built for
one Linux userspace may expect a different dynamic loader, library ABI,
filesystem layout, or set of interfaces. Sharing a kernel family does
not make that executable automatically usable on Android.
The kernel system-call boundary is only one part of the environment an
application expects.

Bionic also shows why ``Linux'' and ``GNU/Linux'' cannot always be treated
as interchangeable labels. GNU's contributions are foundational to many
Linux systems, but Android's system composition differs.
The most informative description names the actual kernel, libraries,
runtime, and application model rather than forcing every product into
one familiar desktop category.

A shell and command tools can exist on Android devices, especially for
development and debugging, without defining the ordinary application
experience. The presence of a command interpreter is not evidence that
a phone offers the same permissions, packages, or workflow as a desktop
distribution. Context determines what the familiar tools can do.

\section{The runtime and framework define application life}
\index{ART}\index{Dalvik}\index{application lifecycle}

Early Android used the Dalvik runtime; later releases adopted ART.
The platform's Java and Kotlin application ecosystem operates through
framework services and a managed runtime, with native code available
through the NDK and other system components. This is a different contract
from launching an arbitrary desktop ELF executable in a login session.

Applications are made of components whose lifetimes are managed by the
system. A visible activity, background work, and a service have different
expectations and restrictions. The platform can reclaim a process,
change an application's execution opportunities, or recreate user
interface state. Exact rules evolve with Android releases and target
SDK levels.

This model reflects resource and user-experience goals.
A phone should remain responsive and conserve energy even when many
applications have been installed. It cannot assume every application
will voluntarily stop work at the ideal moment.
Policy is enforced through framework rules as well as kernel mechanisms.

The lifecycle creates application-design obligations.
Important state cannot live only in a transient screen object.
Work must distinguish what can be repeated, what must survive restart,
and what should be cancelled when the user leaves.
These are variations of the same lifetime and failure questions already
seen in servers and kernels.

\section{Binder supplies a service vocabulary}

Android uses Binder for important communication among processes and
system services. Its object-oriented transaction model provides a way
to invoke operations across process boundaries, transfer references and
certain resources, and associate requests with caller identity.
Generated interfaces can make an interaction resemble a local method
call, but the boundary remains real.

A remote operation can block, fail because a process dies, or trigger
reentrant work. Arguments need serialization and validation.
The service must check whether the caller is authorized to perform the
requested action. A convenient stub does not turn a remote transaction
into an ordinary in-process function call.

Binder's prominence is instructive because it departs from the simplest
``everything is a byte stream'' caricature of Unix.
Android keeps Unix-like processes and descriptors while using a richer
IPC mechanism suited to a service framework. The system did not have to
choose between retaining every historical interface and abandoning the
kernel tradition entirely.

The broader platform also includes facilities such as Zygote for process
startup and shared runtime initialization. These mechanisms exploit
process and memory behavior for a workload very different from the
original shell launching a small command. A stable set of primitive
abstractions can support specialized higher-level launch policies.

\section{The user account becomes an application boundary}
\index{Android!sandbox}\index{permissions!Android}\index{SELinux}

Traditional multiuser Unix often assumed that programs belonging to one
user were mutually trusted. Android ordinarily assigns applications
separate Linux identities and storage boundaries, with exceptions and
shared relationships governed by platform rules. The identity mechanism
is reused to separate applications rather than only human login accounts.

Permissions and framework checks then mediate access to sensitive
capabilities. Modern Android also uses SELinux policy, seccomp and other
hardening, and restrictions on background execution and storage.
The details vary by version and device, but the architectural trend is
clear: a phone's installed applications are not assumed to deserve the
same broad ambient authority.

A permission prompt is not the whole security model.
An application can delegate access, receive a resource through another
component, or interact with a privileged service that validates a request.
Correctness depends on the entire path of authority.
A service that forgets to validate its caller can bypass the intended
boundary even when the application sandbox itself is functioning.

Updates are part of the model too. Kernel, vendor drivers, firmware,
framework components, and applications may have different maintenance
paths. A device's security depends on which components can be updated,
for how long, and by whom. Public source for one layer does not guarantee
that every deployed device receives timely fixes.

\section{Graphics returns to composition}
\index{SurfaceFlinger}

Android applications render into surfaces that are coordinated by the
system's graphics stack. SurfaceFlinger and hardware-composer integration
help assemble what appears on the display. GPU APIs and device drivers
perform work beneath application toolkits.
This is a compositor-centered environment, but it is not simply a Wayland
desktop placed on a phone.

The similarity lies in the engineering problems: buffer ownership,
synchronization, composition, scaling, presentation deadlines, and
hardware capabilities. The protocols and framework arrangements differ.
A family resemblance should not be mistaken for interchangeability.

Touch input also changes policy. Focus, gesture interpretation,
orientation, software keyboards, and application transitions require
coordination across layers. A desktop window-manager convention cannot
be imported unchanged and expected to feel natural.
The user interface embodies the physical form of the device.

Energy makes the frame pipeline a resource policy as well as a visual
one. Rendering unnecessary frames consumes power and generates heat.
The platform must coordinate application demand with refresh behavior,
hardware acceleration, and background limits. Smooth interaction is an
agreement about timing across a whole system.

\section{An open project and a commercial product are different objects}
\index{AOSP}

The Android Open Source Project supplies a large body of platform source.
Commercial devices can combine it with proprietary applications,
services, drivers, firmware, branding, and distribution arrangements.
AOSP and a particular retail phone are therefore not identical artifacts.

The Linux kernel's GPL obligations and the licenses of userspace components
apply to their respective covered material. They do not make every
application and remote service on a phone open source automatically.
A complete account must name which component and which permission is
being discussed.

Control over modification is also practical.
A device may have published source yet restrict bootloader unlocking,
require signed firmware, or depend on unavailable hardware support.
A technically skilled owner may be able to install another system on
some models and not others. The general availability of Android source
does not settle every device's future.

This tension reaches back to the beginning of the book.
Source access, permission to redistribute, ability to replace a component,
and support for a working product have always been separate questions.
Modern devices combine them in new ways rather than making the distinction
obsolete.

\section{BSD and Darwin continue alongside Linux}
\index{FreeBSD}\index{NetBSD}\index{OpenBSD}\index{Mach}\index{XNU}

The contemporary Unix landscape remains plural.
FreeBSD integrates a base system with a strong operating-system identity;
NetBSD has emphasized portability; OpenBSD is associated with security,
code review, and related engineering priorities. These descriptions are
emphases, not exhaustive feature lists or guarantees that every component
of a project has one property.

Apple's Darwin foundation follows another path.
XNU combines Mach-derived facilities with BSD components and other
subsystems, including its driver framework. The shipping macOS experience
adds substantial frameworks, graphics, applications, and product policy
above that foundation. Calling macOS ``just BSD'' or ``just Mach'' misses
the integrated architecture.

The supplied 1986 Mach paper describes an early stage with tasks,
threads, ports, and virtual-memory innovations alongside Unix compatibility.
It should not be used as a literal blueprint for every later Mach-derived
system. In particular, the fact that Mach research explored moving
services into separate servers does not imply that XNU is a pure
user-space-server microkernel \parencite{mach}.

These systems can share tools and standards while preserving distinct
implementation and licensing histories. A shell script may work across
several of them; a binary or kernel module may not.
The book's recurring distinction among API, ABI, ancestry, and certification
is most useful here, where familiar names invite overgeneralization.

\section{From a typesetter to an unseen operating system}

The C/A/T made Unix useful by turning editable source into a printed
artifact people wanted. Android makes a Unix-like kernel useful behind
an interface people do not experience as Unix at all.
The endpoints differ in hardware, audience, scale, and policy, yet both
show the importance of an environment that can be adapted around a
concrete task.

What persisted was not a fixed list of commands or a promise that every
service would become a file. It was a collection of productive boundaries:
processes with identities, named persistent objects, explicit communication,
replaceable tools, and implementations that could travel across machines.
Some boundaries had to be strengthened; others were replaced when their
assumptions no longer fit.

The history also shows that interfaces do not maintain themselves.
People write drivers, review patches, document behavior, resolve licensing
questions, test combinations, and keep old applications alive through
transitions. Their work is easiest to overlook when the result feels
ordinary. A phone that wakes promptly or a game that launches correctly
can conceal decades of such labor.

Unix's future cannot be read from its first edition.
New languages, hardware, security requirements, and forms of interaction
will continue to change what counts as a useful system.
Its history offers something more durable than a prediction: a way to
examine which agreements make a system understandable, which make it
adaptable, and which have become obstacles to the people it serves.
